# Fixture for native_codegen.feature: docs/plans/general-refcounting.md
# Phase 4c -- the compiler-level increment pass (bm_nc_insert_list_
# element_incs, native_codegen.patlang) that makes cascading decrement
# (native_list_rc_element_cascade.patlang) safe to pair with a NAMED,
# independently-tracked local, not just an unnamed temp.
#
# A NAMED local that is ALSO eligible for its own automatic decrement
# (bm_nc_reassignable_eligible_names) has TWO eventual owners once it
# is stored into a list: its own scope-exit decrement, and the
# container's own eventual cascade. Storing the SAME local into TWO
# separate lists makes three: the local's own decrement, and each
# list's own cascade. Without a real increment at each BuildList site
# (one per list, two total here), the refcount would be permanently
# under-counted -- freeing the string the moment the FIRST of the
# three releases fires, leaving the other two to read (or decrement)
# already-freed memory. GitHub #221's own original fix (excluding the
# local from its own decrement entirely) would dodge this specific
# under-count, but at the cost of the string never being reclaimed at
# all; Phase 4c's own increment pass removes that exclusion and relies
# on balancing the count correctly instead (see native_string_rc_dec_
# list_escape.patlang for that single-list case).
#
# Given: a function that builds a string via chr(n), stores it into a
#   named local `s`, stores `s` into TWO separate one-element list
#   literals `a` and `b`, reads `a[0]`/`b[0]` back (to prove no read
#   ever sees corrupted content) and returns whether they match -- `s`,
#   `a` and `b` are then all discarded at the function's own terminal.
# When: called 50000 times.
# Then: the heap's own high-water mark stays flat (no leak: the local's
#   own refcount is correctly balanced at 3 -- its own creation plus
#   two compiler-inserted increments -- against the three releases that
#   actually fire), and every call's own read-back matches (no early
#   free, no double-free, no corruption).
make a function called make_two_lists_sharing takes n returns r
  let s = chr(n % 26 + 65)
  let a = [s]
  let b = [s]
  if a[0] == b[0] then
    return 1
  end
  return 0
end

let loop_count = 50000

make_two_lists_sharing(0)
let before = mem_peek_qword(mem_heap_state_addr(), 0)
let mismatches = 0
let i = 0
while i < loop_count do
  let r = make_two_lists_sharing(i)
  if r != 1 then
    let mismatches = mismatches + 1
  end
  let i = i + 1
end
let after = mem_peek_qword(mem_heap_state_addr(), 0)
print("mismatches= " + mismatches + " growth= " + (after - before))
